{
  "abstract": "Much long-document loss sits on tokens that copy earlier text. We measure how much an exact in-document lookup lowers it on several attention layouts, against spending the same FLOPs on attention. The lookup is ROSA's rule: find the longest 1–4-token context suffix seen earlier in the document; return the token that followed its latest occurrence. Embeddings of that token, match order and distance join the input. 8-layer Transformers, scored in their training format, train for 953 steps of $2^{19}$ tokens on ClimbMix documents of at least 8,193 tokens. Headline contrasts were confirmed once on 673 fresh documents. There the lookup (no added matrix FLOPs) lowers bits per byte on every layout: by .032 / .029 (two seeds) on a 2K-window model, .011 / .008 on an 8K model, and .013 / .008 on an 8K local-global model with 512-token windows. All bootstrap intervals exclude zero. At about matched estimated FLOPs, the local-global model beats the 2K model with the lookup by .005 / .008, unresolved at 477 updates under our .003-bpb rule and .010 at 1,906 (development sets, one seed). On the 2K model most gain comes from copies outside the window; on 8K models much comes from bigrams new to the document. In registered controls, a lookup into another document, which matches less often, gains nothing; on both 8K models a 29K-parameter embedding of match order and distance alone matches the 16.8M-parameter lookup; the 2K model needs the returned token. On packed short documents neither gains reliably. From 477 to 1,906 updates, the 2K model's lookup removes an approximately stable 44–45% of the loss on copies only it can reach, while the 8K model's full and structure-only lookup gains shrink 17–34% per doubling. We document two evaluation pitfalls. Limits: 8 layers, at most 1B tokens, one to four seeds.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "This version makes the four clarifications requested in the review of the previous version. It also adds one registered training-length test that bears on claim 7. Nothing else changes.\n\nReview points (Section 4.4, Table 6d, results/registrations_44.md):\n1. Attention statistic. We now state it exactly: in each head, the attention mass is summed over all earlier positions holding the token, and the maximum of that sum is taken over layers and heads. The table caption and the registration summary use the same wording.\n2. Control token. The text now says that $y'$ has the same count where one exists and the closest count otherwise, and gives the mean counts (27.4 / 27.8 for $y'$ against 29.1 / 29.6 for $y$). We removed \"so that a general sharpening of attention cancels\". The text now says what the control removes and what it does not: the positions of the occurrences, their baseline logits and the maximizing head. The excess is called a descriptive contrast.\n3. Registration scope. The caption of Table 6d and the paragraph after it state that the registered test is seeds 44 and 45 on dev-val. Dev-held-out at every seed is descriptive, as are seeds 46 and 47.\n4. Seed reading. \"Spread, not one atypical seed\" is replaced by the registered thresholds and the observation that neither held. We say that four initialisations cannot establish the distribution or rule out an atypical seed, and that the mean, range and standard deviation are the informative result.\n\nTraining length (Section 4.11, claim 7, abstract, Section 5):\n- Before either run, we registered a test of whether A + G's order-0 gain lasts longer than its copy gain (LEN45). It trains A and A + G at seed 45 for 1,906 updates.\n- Result: r_o = .76 / .82 and r_c = .53 / .56, so the registered reading is that the order-0 gain is more durable, as at seed 44. The order-0 gain still shrinks by 18–24% per doubling, against 36–47% for copies. Two doublings cannot distinguish slow decay from a plateau.\n- The seed gap in the order-0 gain does not close with training (.63 / .62 of seed 44's at 1,906 updates).\n- Table 9 now has seed 45 at 1,906 updates, and Table 10 has seed-45 rows. The per-doubling range for the 8K arms' total gains widens from 17–29% to 17–34%.\n\nSupplement:\n- New files: code/len45_score.py and results/len45.txt.\n- The seed-45 1,906-update records are added to records_hz/horizon.npz, and their result files to runs.json. Their training curves are omitted for size, like those of the other later runs.\n- The LEN45 registration and result are in results/horizon_registrations.md and PROTOCOLS.md.\n- The unused earlier scripts are now xz-compressed (code/tools_v1.txt.xz), to stay within the size limit.\n- All tables reproduce from the extracted zip.",
